1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mstislav_Galitzine
2 CONT https://aimeecrocker.com/people/prince-galitzine-and-the-silver-age/
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Mstislav Alexandrovich Galitzine was born in Kyiv in 1899, the same year as Aimee’s foster daughter Yvonne. Unlike his matrimonial predecessor, Mstislav was a true prince. The Galitzine family of Mstislav's father, Prince A
2 CONC leksandr Mstislavich Golitsyn (Galitzine),  produced its share of generals, novelists and imperial favorites, and, after the revolution, a couple of painters. Mstislav had an inquisitive mind. He studied math, engineering (with th
2 CONC e Zeppelin Company), politics, economics and philosophy.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the outbreak of the Great War, Galitzine enlisted in the Imperial Corps of Pages, a privileged military school reserved for sons of noblemen and high-ranking officers. He went on to fight his own countrymen the “Reds” with Alex
2 CONC ander Kolchak’s “White” army as an officer during the Communist Revolution. He fought on the wrong side of the will of the people. Like many other aristocratic families, his live was changed forever by the Bolsheviks.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aimée (Crocker) Gouraud offered up many explanations as to why she would marry a man more than half her age. She first claimed that the marriage was arranged by destiny. “I had no human desire to marry again, but many years ago I 
2 CONC met a wonderful old seer on top of a high mountain in India who read my whole past and future. He told me that I would marry five times and when Prince Galitzine asked for my hand I realized that it was destiny calling me and I ac
2 CONC cepted. I found that the Prince was a very agreeable young man…” She continued:
2 CONT
2 CONT     "I married a man young enough to be my son because I know I must have the stimulating companionship of youth to maintain my emotionality and vitality. It is always good for a woman old in years to have a young husband, for he 
2 CONC can keep her youthful in spirit."
2 CONT
2 CONT "As an adept of the Hindoo mystics, I have learned to appreciate the value of the vibratory forces that emanate from young bodies and young minds. Long ago I discovered the secret of eternal youth, and this is why I feel as young 
2 CONC today as I did years ago in Samoa when I fell so deeply in love with the late Robert Louis Stevenson, the immortal novelist and poet."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Love, quite as much as youth, is all a matter of invisible waves. Two perfect lovers must have vibrations of equal wave lengths. My training in the mysticism of the East has taught me how to increase or lessen the wave lengths of
2 CONC  my emotions so that they correspond with those of my admirers, and this explains why men have fallen so easily in love with me."
2 CONT
2 CONT The heiress still kept the surname Gouraud but would, when the occasion presented itself, use the title Princess Galitzine for affect for the rest of her life. When asked if the Prince was her fifth or sixth husband she retorted, 
2 CONC “Oh, my dear, no. The Prince is my twelfth husband if I include my matrimonial list of seven Oriental husbands, not registered under the laws of the Occident.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Some said Aimee married the Prince to acquire the title of "Princess".
2 CONT
2 CONT Handsome Prince Galitzine was installed with true princely magnificence at Aimée’s splendid villa in Paris. Troupes of Asian servants waited on him. He received the humble visits of his noble but down-and-out Russian friends on a 
2 CONC special throne-like chair sacred to him alone. Aimée devoted herself to providing for her husband and his comrades, lavish entertainments like they had not known since the revolutionists kicked them out of their palaces. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Prince Mstislav Alexandrovich Galitzine and Aimee (Crocker) Gouraud were married 1 year 7 1/2 months before they divorced in Paris on May 10, 1927.  Aimee would not marry again.
