1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  Russian Prince (and movie star!) Leo Galitzine came to settle in Edson, but who exactly was he?
2 CONT
2 CONT The Galitzine family was one of the oldest and most noble houses in Russia, dating back to the mid-1500’s.
2 CONT
2 CONT The line began with Mikhail Ivanovich Bulgakov, who, in 1514, was involved in the Battle of Orsha, during which he wore an iron glove on one of his hands. This glove soon became his trademark, and after the birth of his son in 155
2 CONC 4, the house of Galista (which translates to Iron Glove) was started. Over the years, the name became Galitzine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Generations later, Prince Aleksander Mstislavich Galitzine, who also held the title of Count Osterman, married Anastasia Komovskaya and together they gave birth to Prince Leo Galitzine in 1905.
2 CONT
2 CONT Twelve years after his birth, the Galitzine’s homeland was swept up in revolution against the Tsarist autocracy. In the early months of 1917, the ruling artisocrat family - the Romanovs – were put under house arrest at Alexandr Pa
2 CONC lace. With the tensions between the peasantry and the nobility increasing every day, many of the royal families of Russia fled to France for safety before they too were captured.
2 CONT
2 CONT While Prince Leo was living in Paris, he met Marguerite Raynaud-Carcas, the daughter of an wealthy Egyptian merchant, and they married in 1928.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1929, the newlyweds arrived in Canada, intending to settle in interior BC to start an airplane company. However, when the couple arrived in Edson, “they became so impressed with its climate and natural advantages” that they dec
2 CONC ided to stay in the area – although they both did invest in small Canadian airlines.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tragedy struck in 1934 when Marguerite fell ill with an appendicitis and later died while travelling in Europe. Leo did not arrive in time for her funeral, as she was buried only 24 hours after her death. The prince visited Egypt 
2 CONC a few years later to file a civil court action for Princess Marguerite Galitzine’s estate, where he was awarded $200,000, which equals about 3.4 million dollars today.
2 CONT
2 CONT After immigrating to the US there is limited documentation of the prince’s travels, places of residence, and occupations. A clearer indication of his occupation reemerged in 1941, when he was cast as an extra in the hit Hollywood 
2 CONC film, The Chocolate Soldier. Although he was only part of the chorus, his aristocratic status drew attention and was used to help promote the film which allowed Leo to meet the star - Eddy Nelson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Leo was also in "The Razor’s Edge", where he played a footman, as well as the 1949 hit, "The Foxes of Harrow". Between his time in major movies, he played an extra in various low budget films being shot in the Los Angeles a
2 CONC rea.
2 CONT
2 CONT While living in California, Leo Galitzine frequently returned to Edson to see the farm that he still owned, and eventually moved to Vancouver, where he continued to write and act in television shows.
2 CONT
2 CONT On December 19, 1969, Leo passed away in Gibsons, B.C., and was buried in the Seaview Cemetery under the name Leo Galitzine-Osterman. Despite his royal heritage, Leo’s obituary in the Coastal Times was very small and did not even 
2 CONC mention his aristocratic lineage. His sister, Ekaterina, was his only surviving family member.
2 CONT
2 CONT Posted by the Galloway Station Museum, Travel Centre & Archives.
