2 CONT '''Marina Dakserhof'''
2 CONT Services will be held tomorrow for Marina Dakserhof, a descendant of Russian nobility, who died Saturday at the age of 95.
2 CONT Mrs. Dakserhof died at her home in San Francisco, surrounded by family and friends.
2 CONT Born in Moscow as Princess Marina Alexadrovna Golitzina, Mrs. Dakserhof lived mostly at the family estate near Petrovskoe, where Napoleon once camped. Her grandfather was governor-general of Moscow and her father was Prince Alexan
2 CONC der Golitzin, a military doctor who was imprisoned in Siberia during the Russian revolution. He fled with his family in a boxcar to Manchuria, later coming to Seattle and settling in Hollywood to continue his medical practice.
2 CONT Mrs. Dakserhof met her husband, the late Boris Dakserhof, while both were living in exile in Seattle. The couple moved to Los Angeles, where Mrs. Dakserhof was active in the Holy Virgin Mary Orthodox Church. Her brother, Alexander
2 CONC  Golitzin, won several Academy Awards for art direction. Her sister, Princess Natasha Golitzina, was a renowned silent film actress.
2 CONT Mrs. Dakserhof, who moved to San Francisco in 1965, provided many scholars with stories about prerevolutionary Russia and life for the Russian community abroad.
2 CONT She was a benefactor of the Russian archives at the Hoover Institution at Stanford University and supported many charities, particularly those involving wild-animal preservation.
2 CONT Besides her brother, Mrs. Dakserhof is survived by two granddaughters and a host of other relatives.
2 CONT The funeral will be held at 11 a.m. at Christ the Savior Russian Orthodox Church, 2040 Anza St. in San Francisco. Flowers may be sent to the church or donations may be made to Defenders of Wildlife, 1101 14th St., NW, Room 1400, W
2 CONC ashington, D.C., 20005.
2 CONT San Francisco Chronicle, Tuesday, September 12, 2000, electronic:
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/228691674/marina_a_dakserhof
